@page "/security"
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Remote support security and POPIA";
    ViewData["Description"] = "End-to-end encrypted remote sessions, two-factor sign-in for everybody, customer consent and unattended passwords, and data kept in South Africa under POPIA.";
}
<section class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h1>Security and POPIA</h1>
        <p class="lede">Remote support software can reach every computer you look after. That is why it is built to be hard to misuse —
            by an outsider, and by anyone who should not be on a particular computer.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2>The sessions</h2>
        <div class="facts" style="margin-top:18px">
            <div class="card fact"><h3>Encrypted end to end</h3><p>A session's screen, keystrokes, files and chat are sealed between the technician's console and the customer's computer (P-256 key agreement, AES-GCM). Our servers pass them on and cannot read them.</p></div>
            <div class="card fact"><h3>Each computer proves who it is</h3><p>Every computer has its own key. If a computer you know turns up with a different one, the console stops and asks before going on.</p></div>
            <div class="card fact"><h3>The customer decides</h3><p>Attended connections need the customer's yes. Unattended access is their switch, with an optional password of their own that nobody — owner included — can get round.</p></div>
            <div class="card fact"><h3>Everything is on the record</h3><p>Every session is its own ticket: who connected, to which computer, when and for how long. A recording is kept only when a technician chooses to make one.</p></div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section alt">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2>Signing in</h2>
        <ul class="feature-list" style="margin-top:18px">
            <li>A code from an authenticator app for every person, at every sign-in</li>
            <li>A trusted computer is asked once a week, not every time</li>
            <li>One sign-in at a time: a second computer signs the first out</li>
            <li>Remove a person and they are signed out at once, everywhere</li>
            <li>Accounts lock after repeated wrong passwords</li>
            <li>Passwords are never stored — only a slow one-way hash</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <div class="wrap grid2">
        <div>
            <h2>Your data, in South Africa</h2>
            <p>Your company's data is kept on servers in South Africa, encrypted on the way there (TLS 1.3) and backed up encrypted every
                night. Each company's data is kept apart from every other's.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
            <h2>POPIA</h2>
            <p>For your customers' information you are the responsible party and we are your operator, as POPIA describes; we use it only to
                provide the service to you. Our <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> and <a href="/terms/ZA">terms</a> say exactly what we
                keep and why. The website uses no analytics and no trackers, and no cookies beyond those that make signing in and its forms work.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
